    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "hey, i'm having some trouble with my sourdough starter consistency. it's been hitting these weird acidity spikes where the pH drops way faster than usual, and it's messing with my dough's extensibility. i'm trying to figure out if this is more related to ambient temp fluctuations in my kitchen or if i need to look at the protein content of my flour blend to stabilize the microbial activity. can you help me break down the relationship between temperature, protein, and the lactobacillus/yeast balance?",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "Variations in ambient temperature can indeed cause fluctuations in the sourdough fermentation process, affecting yeast and lactobacillus activity, which in turn can impact the consistency of your starter and dough. Warmer temperatures typically increase the activity of both organisms, possibly leading to more rapid pH decreases. Protein content can also affect the fermentation, as different proteins can influence the structure and strength of the gluten network, altering dough extensibility. Balancing protein levels in your flour while maintaining a stable temperature in your starter environment can help stabilize microbial activity and keep acidity more consistent. Consider using a cold spot in your kitchen for your starter and experimenting with different flour blends to find the optimal balance.",
